what's good everybody Michael from  GameSpot here at the Sony headquarters  in San Mateo California checking out the  upcoming PlayStation VR games let's  check it out let's go  [Music]  this is the first look anyone's ever had  of the game the persistence which is a  first-person survival horror psvr game  that plays very similarly to Resident  Evil 7 best that kind of reminds me of  System Shock almost where you're going  through a spaceship with humans that  have been transformed into horrible  monsters and one really nice feature  about this is that you can have four of  your friends jump on through a companion  app that's available on any tablet or  smartphone and they can control anything  within your environment so they have the  option to either assist you throughout  the ship or they can screw around with  you and trick you or or mess up your  playthrough and make it even more  challenging generally it makes for a fun  more inclusive experience with VR though  it was a bear experience at first with  only sparse enemies and linear  environments I do expect that the game  will flesh out a little bit more as you  move on the next game I got to play was  star blood arena which is a competitive  shooter game where you're in a vessel  and you have multiple weapons in a  three-dimensional space you can go up  down left right and move throughout the  environment to play it essentially a  deathmatch type of game I got to hook up  with a couple of the developers to play  an eight-player deathmatch and it's a  pretty straightforward experience where  you get different loadouts you can  customize your characters put decals on  your flying mechs the only problem I  would have with this is that if you're  susceptible to motion sickness in VR  this may be a tough experience to have  since there is a lot of movement along  the x and y and z-axes  at a very fast pace but if you're not  susceptible to motion sickness this may  be an interesting psvr experience  fantastic contraption has been out on  oculus and vibe for a while now but it's  finally coming to psvr  what's different about the psvr version  is that since we don't have room scale  experiences we can now change the size  of our playground and zoom in on  different areas of the map and control  and rotate it with our PS Move  controllers so we still get that full  game without having to compromise with  the fact that we don't have room scale  so if you're familiar with this game you  play with the set of tools and you build  different vehicles and well contraptions  to move an object to a certain goal it  was one of the highlights of VR last  year and you can experience this now in  PlayStation VR one game I play that  really stood out was static which is a  VR puzzle based game with with a device  strapped to your hand and you really  have to figure out what the controls do  throughout each puzzle what I really  appreciated about this game is that it  didn't tell you anything up front it was  up to you to experiment not just with  the controls but with what the game  requires you to solve in terms of the  puzzles it took me about a good 10 15  minutes to not only get a grasp of the  controls but figure out which what each  aspect of the puzzle does and what  you're asked to do  it's almost reminiscent of the witness  where you're dumped into this world to  figure things out on your own  and the aesthetic almost reminds me of  portal where it's very sterile and a lab  type experience I do expect the game to  change up its mechanics and its level  design and what it asks you to do  throughout the game but we'll find out  when it does come out far point is  PlayStation VR is much-anticipated  sci-fi shooter and we got to play a new  level here today using the PlayStation  VR aim controller which is new  peripheral designed to simulate an  actual gun the peripheral itself is  fairly lightweight it's accurate and  it's tracking and has all the full set  of buttons it works well 